---
description: Conventions and known traps when editing kernel C or assembly
globs: ["kernel/**/*.c", "kernel/**/*.h", "kernel/**/*.asm", "kernel/linker.ld", "Makefile"]
---

Comments carry the reasoning, not a restatement of the code: the alternative
that was rejected, or the bug that produced the current shape. Match the
density of the surrounding file. British spelling.

Bring-up order in `kmain.c` is a dependency graph written as a sequence. The
filesystem allocates, so it follows the heap; it timestamps, so it follows the
clock; the graphics adapter's registers are at PCI-assigned addresses, so it
follows paging. A subsystem used before it is ready does not raise an error
here — it returns zeroes.

Traps this codebase has already fallen into. Each is written up in
`CHALLENGES.md`; these are the ones that will recur:

- The multiboot header must land in the first 8 KB of the image. An
  auto-inserted `.note.gnu.build-id` pushed it out once and GRUB refused the
  kernel. `-Wl,--build-id=none` and a `/DISCARD/` entry keep it there.
- Assembly objects take a `.asm.o` suffix, or `isr.c` and `isr.asm` both
  produce `isr.o` and silently overwrite each other.
- Send the interrupt acknowledgement *before* calling the handler. The timer
  handler ends in a context switch and never returns.
- `PAGE_USER` is required on the page directory entry, not only the table
  entry. Permission is an AND across levels.
- Never poll hardware in an unbounded loop. An absent serial port reads as
  permanently busy and hangs the kernel in its first `kprintf`. Probe, then
  bound the wait.
- Never ask a device where the cursor is. `vga_get_x()` stopped moving when the
  framebuffer console arrived and a padding loop ran for ever. Use
  `console_column()`.
- The compiler deletes undefined behaviour. A deliberate divide-by-zero has to
  be inline assembly.

The Makefile globs `kernel/*.c`, so a new source file needs no build change.
